autonomous driving is a big  technological challenge  probably the biggest of our generation  this is a tesla it comes with a feature  known as  autopilot and elon musk says in the  future it'll have a feature called  full self driving several other cars  being sold today also come with assisted  driving technology  which still requires someone behind the  wheel but new vehicles that won't have  anyone in the driver's seat are getting  closer to production  startups waymo cruise too simple and  aurora  are already testing driverless tech on  some public roads across  america it's going to move quickly we'll  start to see this  move from prototypes to actually scaling  products into the world  we see us becoming fully driverless in  2024  as they invest billions in r d and sign  multi-billion dollar deals  their valuations have soared and too  simple has gone public  they're pitching a future where people  won't even own cars younger generations  won't even think about  owning cars and some jobs will no longer  exist we fundamentally believe that  every trucker  will be able to retire as a trucker but  first of all they need to convince  governments and the public that their  technology is safe you're limiting your  potential if consumers are not  comfortable getting into your vehicle  and taking a ride  so could these startups make driving a  thing of the past  tesla has been working on making its  vehicles autonomous since around 2016  and said it planned to have self-driving  cars on the road by 2020.  and sometimes i'm not on time but  i get it done for now experts say these  cars have what they call level 2  autonomy  it is a level 2 system where they're  saying that the driver has to be there  they have to pay attention they're  counting on them  what does that mean well it's a scale  used by auto engineers and it starts at  zero which means  you know kind of a 1950s car with no  automation at all  and goes up to level five basically when  the car  can do everything anywhere you can drop  it off  in afghanistan today  and it will be able to operate without  maps by itself  instead of releasing vehicles and  working towards autonomy  these top startups want to roll them out  directly at level 4  autonomy which is where the car doesn't  need a driver as long as it's pre-loaded  with information about its surroundings  like maps and directions and we just  simply think  level 4 is the goal for any  domain some companies in the us and asia  like baidu  auto x and dd say they've reached level  4 in some vehicles though none have  rolled them out at commercial scale  but some are getting close waymo and  cruz have piloted a fleet of robo taxis  in some ring-fenced areas around the us  think ride-hailing without the driver  like this the waymo won  we're the only company that has a fully  autonomous ride-hailing service  available to the public today in the  phoenix metro area  in late 2020 waymo began removing  support drivers from its vehicles after  its cars racked up some 20 million miles  on public roads  gathering troves of data for its  algorithms the car shows up completely  empty  they take a ride from point a to point b  that's one of the ways you make this  real  is you launch a service in the city and  you see if customers will actually adopt  a service and use it  waymo started as a google project in  2009  and now has a 30 billion valuation an  alphabet google's parent  as a majority shareholder cruz has  roughly the same valuation as waymo and  is owned by gm  with whom it has built an autonomous  vehicle from scratch it has accrued  fewer miles in waymo some 2 million  in its tests in san francisco miles  driven absolutely  matters because ultimately the goal is  to put  this technology on public roads  interacting with other human driven  vehicles so you do need  that experience at some point to say  yes this is not only safe but it's  actually viable  crews recently got permission to remove  the support driver 